    en: { title: 'A slower day', subtitle: 'Notes on small things', text: `The morning began without a list. On the windowsill, a glass of water held a small patch of sky. Someone downstairs was opening a shop. The sound of the shutter rose through the quiet, then settled into the ordinary hum of the street.

I took a book to the table and left my phone in another room. There was nothing especially important about this decision. I wanted to finish a page without discovering, halfway through it, that I had gone somewhere else.

Outside, a bicycle leaned against the wall. Its owner came back carrying bread in a paper bag. A few leaves crossed the pavement in the wind. These things had probably happened every morning. Today there was enough room to notice them.

Reading asks for a particular kind of attention. It does not always arrive as concentration. Sometimes it begins with a comfortable chair, a little daylight, and the willingness to stay with a sentence until its meaning becomes clear.

At noon I made tea. The kettle clicked off, and for a moment the whole room seemed to be listening. I put a small spoon beside the cup and returned to the paragraph I had left unfinished. It was still there, waiting without impatience.

The book described a journey, but I was content not to take one. The shade moved slowly across the floor. A neighbour watered the plants on the balcony. In the distance a bus stopped, opened its doors, and carried on.

By the afternoon, I had read less than I expected and remembered more. There was a sentence I wanted to keep. I read it again, then once more, as if trying the weight of a stone in my hand before putting it in my pocket.

Nothing remarkable happened that day. There was no photograph to prove that it had been worthwhile. When evening came, I closed the book and left it on the table. A little space remained in the room, and I decided not to fill it.` },
