    en: { title: 'Walking the city', subtitle: 'A route without a destination', text: `I turned left where I usually turned right. That was the whole plan for the afternoon. The city had not changed, but the order in which I met it had, and for a while this was enough to make familiar streets feel new.

A narrow passage ran between two buildings. At the other end was a courtyard with a single tree and several chairs that did not match. Someone had left a newspaper folded on one of them. The shade was almost perfectly still.

Maps are good at telling us how places connect. They are less good at describing the way a street smells after rain, or the small hesitation before we step into a shop we have never noticed before. Those details belong to walking.

I passed a bakery, a repair shop, and a window full of plants. In the repair shop, a man was examining a small clock. He held it close to his ear, then set it down with the care usually reserved for something alive.

At a crossing, I waited beside a woman carrying flowers. We both watched a bird hop along the railing. The light changed. We went in different directions, carrying away our separate versions of the same brief moment.

There are routes that become so familiar we stop seeing them. A change of pace can bring them back. Walk a little slower, stop one corner earlier, look up at the windows instead of down at the pavement.

Near the river I found a bench and opened my book. The noise of traffic softened behind the trees. I read a few pages, then watched the water for longer than I intended. Neither activity seemed to interrupt the other.

When I finally went home, the distance was not very great. I had not discovered a secret part of the city. I had simply spent an afternoon paying attention to the part that was already there.` },
